    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p32-q3a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Prove that\n$$m(m!)=(m+1)!-m!.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$$(m+1)!-m!=((m+1)-1)m!=m(m!).$$",
          "worked": "Starting with the right-hand side:\n$$\\begin{align*}\n(m+1)!-m!&=(m+1)m!-m!\\\\\n(m+1)!-m!&=((m+1)-1)m!\\\\\n(m+1)!-m!&=m(m!).\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-q3b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, prove that\n$$1(1!)+2(2!)+\\cdots+n(n!)=(n+1)!-1.$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$$1(1!)+2(2!)+\\cdots+n(n!)=(n+1)!-1.$$\n\nUse part (a) to write each term as a difference of consecutive factorials; the intermediate terms cancel.",
          "worked": "Using part (a), then cancelling consecutive factorials:\n$$\\begin{align*}\n1(1!)+2(2!)+\\cdots+n(n!)&=(2!-1!)+(3!-2!)+\\cdots+((n+1)!-n!)\\\\\n1(1!)+2(2!)+\\cdots+n(n!)&=(n+1)!-1!\\\\\n1(1!)+2(2!)+\\cdots+n(n!)&=(n+1)!-1.\n\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
  },
